                       ORDER PARTIALLY GRANTING JOINT MOTION
                          TO EXTEND RATE SUSPENSION PERIOD

          THIS MATTER comes before the Co-Hearing Examiners upon New Mexico Large

Customer Group’s (“NMLCG”) and Louisiana Energy Service’s (“LES”) (collectively, the “Joint

Movants”) Joint Motion to Extend Rate Suspension Period (the “Joint Motion”), and Southwestern

Public Service Company’s (“SPS”) Response to New Mexico Large Customer Group and

Louisiana Energy Services LLC’s Joint Motion to Extend Rate Suspension Period (“Response”).

The Co-Hearing Examiners, having reviewed the Joint Motion and Response, FIND AND

CONCLUDE:

          1.      On February 27, 2026, the Joint Movants filed the Joint Motion requesting that the

New Mexico Public Regulation Commission (“Commission”) extend the statutory suspension

period applicable to SPS’s Advice Notice No. 341 by an additional three months to align the

effective date of new rates with the requirements of the Commission’s Future Test Year Rule

(“FTY Rule”), which provides that a future test year must begin “no later than the date a proposed

rate change is expected to take effect.” 1



1
    See Rule 17.1.3.7(G); see also NMSA 1978, § 62-3-3(P).
                       BEFORE THE NEW MEXICO PUBLIC REGULATION COMMISSION
Order Partially Granting Joint Motion                                                 Docket No. 25-00079-UT
to Extend Rate Suspension Period


            2.     In this case, SPS uses a Future Test Year (“FTY”) running from December 1, 2026,

through November 30, 2027. 2

            3.     The Joint Movants noted that the Commission’s December 11, 2025, Order

Suspending Advice Notice 341(“Suspension Order”) suspended the rate effective date for an initial

period of nine months, until September 20, 2026, 3 and stated that “without extending the rate

suspension period an additional three months, the proposed rates’ effective date would begin in

September 2026, three months before SPS’s proposed FTY.” 4

            4.     The Joint Movants asserted that the “current Suspension Order is inconsistent with

the FTY rule and section 62-3-3(P) of the [Public Utility] Act because SPS’s FTY is set to begin

three months later than the date SPS’s proposed rate increase is expected to take effect,” 5 and the

“discrepancy is grounds for invoking the Commission’s mandate under section 62-8-7(C) of the

[Public Utility] Act to ‘extend the [suspension] period for an additional three months’ because ‘a

longer time[is] required ‘to comply with the statutory and regulatory framework.” 6

            5.     The Joint Movants therefore requested that the Commission extend the rate

suspension period to December 20, 2026. 7




