Order Partially Granting Joint Motion                                          Docket No. 25-00079-UT
to Extend Rate Suspension Period


           6.       On March 6, 2026, SPS filed its Response, in which SPS stated it “does not oppose

an extension period to ensure that SPS’s new rates do not take effect before the start of the FTY,

and … agrees that the FTY should align with the date when SPS’s new rates go into effect.” 8

           7.       SPS additionally noted that the FTY begins on December 1, 2026, and requested

that the suspension period align with that date. 9 SPS asserted “there is no good cause to extend the

suspension period beyond [November 30, 2026], and doing so would unnecessarily delay SPS’s

ability to recover costs under its new rates.” 10

           8.       SPS therefore requested “that the Commission extend the suspension period only

to the extent necessary to ensure that the effective date of the new rates does not precede the

beginning of the FTY,” and asserted that “[d]oing so would appropriately balance the interests of

SPS, its customers, and the public in accordance with the requirements of the Public Utility Act.” 11

           9.       Having reviewed the Joint Motion and the Response, the Co-Hearing Examiners

find that it is necessary to extend the rate suspension period to ensure that new rates do not take

effect before the start of the FTY, and that extending the rate suspension period until November

30, 2026, reasonably aligns with the Public Utility Act.

           10.      Further, the Commission may extend the rate suspension period as far as December

20, 2026, if at a future date it determines that a longer time will be required.




